ABOUT: Welcome to the commentary track!

rtn:V-VERSION: Print the title, release, and serial number.
	In 1982, Infocom briefly branded its games as “INTERLOGIC”. They had mostly switched to “interactive fiction” by 1984.


GRAMMAR-LEGEND: This is the list of verb forms which the game recognizes. (Simple directions like `NORTH` are a special case; they’re not listed here.) These are compiled from the [SYNTAX|~src:SYNTAX-67] file.
	Objects in a verb line have various flags which give hints about their usage. An object attribute (like `LIGHTBIT`) means that if the player doesn’t name an object, the parser will try to guess one with that attribute.
	The other flag letters have specific meanings: the object must be **h**eld; the object will be **t**aken if not held; the object should be **i**n the room or **o**n the ground; the object should be **p**ossessed or **c**arried in a container. (Yes, they’re confusing. Infocom’s own docs say “No one really understands them except Stu” (Galley).)

attr:RMUNGBIT: Not applied to real objects. `RMUNGBIT` is a hack used in grammar lines; it means the object is optional (but the preposition before it is required). This allows the parser to accept commands like [LOOK AROUND|~src:SYNTAX-279].

glob:P-SYNTAX: The grammar line found when the most recent command was parsed. (Except for simple direction commands, which are a special case.) See the Grammar tab.


OBJTREE-LEGEND: In this list, `Rooms` are rooms and `Items` are regular items. (Sometimes portable, sometimes not.)
	`Scen` indicates scenery objects. These exist in multiple places; for example, the [~obj:HOUSE] is visible from most of the outdoors areas.
	`Glob` indicates a global object, which is *always* visible, or at least mentionable. For example, [~obj:GLOBAL-MURDER] is global, so you can always `ASK PERSON ABOUT MURDER`.

obj:LOCAL-GLOBALS: This is a container for scenery objects like [~obj:HOUSE], which are visible from many locations in the game.


glob:WINNER: `WINNER` is the actor in the current command.
	`WINNER` is usually [~obj:PLAYER], but for a command like `GEORGE, GO EAST` it briefly shifts to the named NPC.

glob:HERE: The location of the [*glob:WINNER].

glob:SCORE: The hours value of the current time. (See also [glob:PRESENT-TIME].)
	The Z-machine was originally designed to show `SCORE` and `MOVES` in the status line. In *Deadline*, these variables were repurposed to show a time in hours and minutes. A bit in the game file header tells the interpreter which way to format them.

glob:MOVES: The minutes value of the current time. (See also [glob:PRESENT-TIME].)
	The Z-machine was originally designed to show `SCORE` and `MOVES` in the status line. In *Deadline*, these variables were repurposed to show a time in hours and minutes. A bit in the game file header tells the interpreter which way to format them.

glob:PRESENT-TIME: The current time, stored as minutes after midnight (`H*60+M`). The game starts at 8:00 am.
	See also [glob:SCORE] and [glob:MOVES], which track the current time in a different way.
	
src:ACTIONS-109: The [glob:PRESENT-TIME] global is defined twice, which is not an error in ZIL.
	(The comment is wrong, though. Maybe it’s on Daylight Saving Time?)

rtn:GO: Set up the world at the start of the game. Start the timers. Print the banner. Set all those useful globals like [~glob:HERE] and [~glob:WINNER].

rtn:WELCOME: Why do you have exactly twelve hours to solve the murder? At least one reason is so that the game does not have to parse “am” or “pm” times. `WAIT UNTIL 9:30` unambiguously refers to the morning, because you can’t stay until 9:30 in the evening.

glob:LIT: Whether the current location has light. *Deadline* has no dark areas so this is always true.

glob:DEAD: It is possible to die in *Deadline*, but this variable is never used.

glob:LOW-DIRECTION: Properties with this value and above are directions.

glob:LOAD-MAX: The weight limit you can carry. This is always 100.

glob:LOAD-ALLOWED: Your current weight limit. This is a carry-over from the Zork games, where you can carry less when injured. The [~rtn:ITAKE] routine checks this condition, but nothing in the game can injure you.

glob:P-WON: Set if the parser successfully parsed a command. (As opposed to a blank line or an error.)
	No relation to whether the *player* has won.

glob:P-ADVERB: Unlike other Infocom games (and nearly all modern IF), *Deadline* pays attention to some adverbs. You can `EXAMINE` an object, or you can `EXAMINE` it `CAREFULLY`. The parser will also [take note|~src:PARSER-265] of the words `QUIETLY`, `SLOWLY`, `QUICKLY`, and `BRIEFLY`.
	`EXAMINE CAREFULLY` takes several minutes instead of one, but in a few cases it reveals more information. See [~rtn:NOTE-PAPER-F], [~rtn:NEWSPAPER-F], [~rtn:BOOKSHELVES-F].
	The only other meaningful adverb is `CLIMB STAIRS QUIETLY`, which [informs you|~src:VERBS-339] that it really is impossible.

src:PARSER-265: Unlike other Infocom games (and nearly all modern IF), *Deadline* pays attention to some adverbs. See [glob:P-ADVERB].

glob:C-INTS: Indicates how much of the timer table ([*glob:C-TABLE]) is used. This counts down from `C-TABLELEN`, which is 300.

glob:C-DEMONS: Indicates how much of the timer table is used for daemons.
	The difference between daemons and timers is that timers run every turn; daemons run every *input*, even if no turn passes. But *Deadline* does not use daemons.

glob:C-TABLE: The timer table. See the “Timers” tab for a current listing.

TIMERS-LEGEND: A list of the timers running (or paused) in the timer table ([~glob:C-TABLE]). Positive numbers count down (while enabled) and call the named function after that many turns. Negative numbers are called every turn while the timer is enabled.
	This list only grows as you play; timers can be added, started, and stopped, but not removed.
	Note that enabled negative timers, like [~rtn:I-ATTENTION], count down towards “negative infinity”. They’ll wrap and stop working after 65535 turns, but back then nobody worried about this!


obj:OBJECT-PAIR: Multiple actions like `GET ALL` are usually handled one object at a time. However, *Deadline* allows you to `ARREST` two people as one action. (Yeah, that counts as a spoiler, sorry.)
	The [~rtn:MAIN-LOOP] has a special case for this [here|~src:MAIN-93]. The `ARREST` action is performed with the special object `OBJECT-PAIR`, whose action function [rtn:OBJECT-PAIR-F] then handles the dual arrest.

rtn:OBJECT-PAIR-F: The code which handles an action on two objects at once. (See [obj:OBJECT-PAIR].) In *Deadline*, this is only the `ARREST` action. So this routine contains all the logic for arresting two people.
	For a complete breakdown of the endgame logic, see [SOLUTION].

obj:GLOBAL-WEATHER: This is a generic topic object. It’s loaded up with synonyms like `PROBLEM` and `WRONG` so that you can `ASK GEORGE ABOUT PROBLEM` or `ROURKE, WHATS WRONG?`
	Rourke responds to that last with “I don’t know nothing about no weather,” and now you know why.

