glob:ANALYSIS-OBJ: Whether Duffy’s current task is `FINGERPRINT` or `ANALYZE`.

src:ACTIONS-2709: McNabb will need the ladder in the [~obj:ORCHARD] around noon. (See [I-GARDENER|~src:GOAL-512].) This bit of business appears to be meant to avoid the awkwardness of McNabb grabbing the ladder while it’s off at the police lab.


glob:MOVEMENT-GOALS: The day’s schedule for each character.
	Each character (other than the player and Coates) has a list of movement times and goals. A line `30 10 DINING-ROOM` means that they will wait 30±10 minutes (20 to 40) and then begin moving towards the `DINING-ROOM`.

glob:GOAL-TABLES: The current movement goal of each character. The scheme is explained in the “Schedule” tab.

rtn:IN-MOTION?: Whether a character is moving towards a destination, as determined by [glob:GOAL-TABLES].

rtn:IMOVEMENT: Prepare a character to move towards their next destination. The character’s timer routine calls this when it is time for them to “wake up” and move.
	This calls [rtn:ESTABLISH-GOAL] to set up their movement. Then they advance one line on their [~glob:MOVEMENT-GOALS] table and set a long timer (30 or 60 minutes or whatever) – the delay until their *next* wake-up call.

# More precisely:
# - if not first move (initial 0 entry), establish-goal
# - if not last entry (final 0 time), pick the interval, queue timer, advance ptr

rtn:I-FOLLOW: Move each character towards their current goal, if they ought to be in motion.

rtn:FOLLOW-GOAL: Move a character one step towards their current goal. All the work driven by the [~glob:GOAL-TABLES] happens in this routine.

rtn:ESTABLISH-GOAL: Set up a character’s [~glob:GOAL-TABLES] entry to move them towards a destination. Normally this is driven by the [~glob:MOVEMENT-GOALS] table. If the `PRIORITY` argument is set, this is a plot-critical action which overrides the normal schedule.
	See the “Schedule” tab for a complete explanation.

rtn:INT-WAIT: Run the game time forward by `N` minutes. Returns true if the full time elapsed, or false if the player chose to stop waiting (because of some observed action).
	This is used by a couple of actions that take more than one minute: `EXAMINE CAREFULLY`, `EXAMINE ROOM`. If you don’t wait the full interval, the `EXAMINE` action fails.

prop:LINE: The transit line which a given room is on or adjacent too. See [~prop:STATION].

prop:STATION: The transit-line room which is, or is adjacent to, a given room. See [~prop:LINE].
	([~obj:GUEST-ROOM] and [~obj:GEORGE-BATH] are not adjacent to their `STATION` rooms. This seems to be a mistake, but it doesn’t matter because NPCs never travel to those rooms.)

glob:TOP-OF-THE-LINE: These tables define the four “transit lines” which let NPCs travel around the map. Every room is either on or adjacent to a transit line.

src:GOAL-37: `SHALL-3` is not a valid room name. (The South Hall contains only [~obj:SHALL-1] and [~obj:SHALL-2].) What happened here?
	In the compiled table, this value is zero. It seems that this was a double bug: a mistake in the *Deadline* source that went unnoticed because the ZIL compiler failed to flag it as an undefined symbol.
	Since characters only use the transit tables to move to specific rooms, this bad table row doesn’t cause any game problems.
	(It’s possible that an early draft of the map had a `SHALL-3` room which was removed. That is speculation, however.)

rtn:I-ATTENTION: This timer routine decreases each character’s attention level after you call their name or do something else which calls [~rtn:GRAB-ATTENTION]. These values are stored in [glob:ATTENTION-TABLE]. After two to six minutes, they will become impatient and leave.

glob:ATTENTION-TABLE: The current attention level of each character. This is set positive if you call their name or do something else which calls [~rtn:GRAB-ATTENTION]. It then decreases every turn ([rtn:I-ATTENTION]) to zero, at which point they wander off.
	(In fact the values in this table decrease *every* turn, becoming negative. Negative values are ignored. For simplicity, I display negative values as “0” in the Schedule tab.)

glob:COR-1: The twelve `COR-N` tables define twelve corridors of rooms that have sight-lines to each other. (Some of the “corridors” are outdoors.) Each room in a corridor has that bit set in its [prop:CORRIDOR] property.
	This permits the game to display messages like “Mrs. Robner, off to the north, ducks into a room to the north.”

prop:CORRIDOR: This property defines one or more “corridors” (sight-lines) that the player can see down from a given room. See [glob:COR-1].

glob:WHERE-TABLES: This tracks when each character last saw each other character. This lets you ask questions like `DUNBAR, WHERE IS GEORGE?` (You won’t get the location, but you will get a time.)

rtn:I-BAXTER-ARRIVE: As you might expect, this timer causes Baxter to appear at 9:55 am. Thereafter he is controlled by [glob:MOVEMENT-GOALS] like the other characters.

src:GOAL-449: When Baxter reaches the South Lawn, he leaves the game.

rtn:I-COATES-ARRIVE: As you might expect, this timer causes Coates to appear at 11:51-11:55 am.

src:ACTIONS-2157: At the end of the [~rtn:WELCOME] sequence, Mrs. Robner heads to the kitchen. The [~obj:KITCHEN] is not listed in her [~glob:MOVEMENT-GOALS] for the day; this is a special case. She’ll return to her schedule at the next listed time.

rtn:I-WINDOW-LOOK: If you `LOOK OUT WINDOW` in the [~obj:GUEST-ROOM], you can see the [~obj:EAST-LAWN]. The game handles this by setting the room’s [prop:CORRIDOR] property to 512, thus giving it line-of-sight via [~glob:COR-512] – but only for a turn. After that, you stop paying attention to the window.

glob:COR-512: This one is conditional. From the [~obj:GUEST-ROOM], you only notice movement on the [~obj:EAST-LAWN] if you explicitly `LOOK OUT WINDOW`. See [rtn:I-WINDOW-LOOK].


glob:LADDER-FLAG: Set if the ladder is in [~obj:IN-ROSES] and leaning against the [~obj:LIBRARY-BALCONY].

glob:LADDER-FLAG-2: Set if the ladder is in [~obj:IN-ORCHARD] and leaning against the [~obj:BEDROOM-BALCONY].

glob:LADDER-POSITIONED: This is set exactly when [glob:LADDER-FLAG] is set, and for the same reason. This variable is not used and appears to be vestigial.


rtn:I-G-I-G: The timer for McNabb noticing (and raging about) the garden holes. This is queued up when he first enters the [~obj:ROSE-GARDEN].

glob:G-I-G: The time at which McNabb noticed the garden holes.

glob:HOLE-TELL: Whether McNabb has told you about the garden holes.

rtn:I-SHOW-HOLE: The timer for McNabb showing you the holes after you ask him to. If you don’t follow him into the garden right away, he gets sarcastic. If delay more than a few minutes, he gives you up as a bad job ([glob:NO-SHOW]) and you lose your chance.

glob:NO-SHOW: Whether McNabb has gotten irate at you for wasting his time after you asked him to show you the holes.

glob:HOLE-SHOWN: Whether McNabb has shown you the garden holes.


glob:ENVELOPE-OPENED: Whether you have opened the envelope. (See also [glob:MR-ENVELOPE].)

glob:MR-ENVELOPE: Whether Mrs. Robner *knows* you opened the envelope. (See also [glob:ENVELOPE-OPENED].)


glob:NOTE-READ: Whether you have discovered the impressions on the [~obj:NOTE-PAPER].

glob:B-NOTE: Whether you have shown Baxter the impressions discovered on the [~obj:NOTE-PAPER].

glob:B-FOCUS: Whether you have shown Baxter the [~obj:BAXTER-PAPERS].


glob:BOOKS-MOVED: Whether George has disarranged the books in the library. (See also [glob:GEORGE-MOVES-BOOKS].)

glob:GEORGE-MOVES-BOOKS: Whether you’ve *seen* George disarrange the books in the library. (See also [glob:BOOKS-MOVED].)


glob:STUB-D: Whether you’ve heard Dunbar’s version of the concert night.

glob:STUB-B: Whether you’ve heard Baxter’s version of the concert night.

glob:STUB-DX: Set if Baxter heard you asking Dunbar about the concert. He adjusts his story to match hers.

glob:STUB-BX: Set if Dunbar heard you asking Baxter about the concert. She adjusts her story to match his.

glob:CONTRADICTION: Whether you have discovered the inconsistency between Baxter’s story of the concert and Dunbar’s version.
	While this is a key point in the case, the game does not check the `CONTRADICTION` flag! (It does check [glob:STUB-D].)


glob:G-CALENDAR: Whether you have showed George the calendar page which says “Will completed.”
	This kicks off the sequence where George enters the hidden closet, removes the [~obj:NEW-WILL], and throws it into the lake. See [rtn:GEORGE-HACK].
	
rtn:GEORGE-HACK: Send George off to find the [~obj:NEW-WILL]. This is called when George learns about both the new will ([glob:G-CALENDAR]) and the fact that it’s missing (at the noon reading).
	[rtn:I-GEORGE-HACK] handles George’s next moves.

rtn:I-GEORGE-HACK: This handles George’s trip to [~obj:GEORGE-ROOM].
	When he reaches his room, he will wait for an opportunity to sneak into the [~obj:LIBRARY]; see [rtn:I-GEORGE-HACK-2].

rtn:I-GEORGE-HACK-2: A short timer which handles George hiding in his room, waiting for you to leave so that he can sneak into the [~obj:LIBRARY]. When he reaches the Library, [rtn:I-GEORGE-HACK-3] begins.

rtn:I-GEORGE-HACK-3: This handles George’s search of the [~obj:LIBRARY] and the [~obj:HIDDEN-CLOSET]. When he retrieves the [~obj:NEW-WILL], his destination is set to the [~obj:NORTH-LAWN], where he will [dispose of it|~src:GOAL-606].


glob:LOBLO-FLAG: Whether you have shown Dunbar the [~obj:LAB-REPORT] that shows her medication.
	This kicks off the sequence where Dunbar arranges a meeting with Baxter in the shed. See [rtn:DUNBAR-SEQUENCE].

rtn:I-DUNBAR-ACTIVATE: A short timer that begins when you show Dunbar the [~obj:LAB-REPORT]. It invokes [rtn:DUNBAR-SEQUENCE] if you haven’t already accused her.

rtn:DUNBAR-SEQUENCE: Send Dunbar off looking for Baxter. This is called a few minutes after you show Dunbar the [~obj:LAB-REPORT], or immediately if you `ACCUSE` her at that point.
	[rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ] handles Dunbar’s next moves.

glob:DUNBAR-ACCUSED: Whether [rtn:DUNBAR-SEQUENCE] has begun.

glob:D-S-BAXTER-LOC: The location where Dunbar hopes to find Baxter when she is searching for him. See [rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ].

rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ: This handles Dunbar searching the house for Baxter. She travels to his last known location ([~glob:D-S-BAXTER-LOC]). If he’s not there, she keeps trying.
	Once Dunbar and Baxter meet, [rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-2] is queued up in five minutes. (Unless you interrupt them, in which case they stop and wait for you to leave.)
	### bug if she doesn’t find him the first time?

rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-2: Sends Dunbar off to the [~obj:SHED-ROOM], where she will wait for Baxter; see [rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-D].
	Also queues Baxter to head outside after ten minutes; see [rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-3].

rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-D: This handles Dunbar’s trip to the [~obj:SHED-ROOM]. She is nervous and may drop the [~obj:STUB] along the way.
	When both Baxter and Dunbar are in the Shed, [rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-4] begins.

rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-3: Sends Baxter off to the [~obj:NORTH-LAWN]. See [rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-B].
	
rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-B: This handles Baxter’s walk to the [~obj:NORTH-LAWN].
	When he gets there, he will head towards the [~obj:SHED-ROOM]; see [rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-B-2].

rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-B-2: This handles Baxter’s walk to the [~obj:SHED-ROOM].
	When both Baxter and Dunbar are in the Shed, [rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-4] begins.

rtn:I-DUNBAR-SEQ-4: Handles the meeting between Baxter and Dunbar in the [~obj:SHED-ROOM].
	This ends after five minutes, or if you interrupt them in the middle of their argument. See [rtn:HEAD-FOR-CLIMAX].

glob:SECRET-MEETING: How long Baxter and Dunbar have been conversing in the shed.

### MEETING-INTERRUPTED: always set when the meeting ends? only affects ARREST DUNBAR outcome.

rtn:HEAD-FOR-CLIMAX: Like the [comment|~src:ACTIONS-3552] says, this is the big finish. Dunbar heads for [~obj:DUNBAR-ROOM]; Baxter heads for the [~obj:NORTH-LAWN] where he will do his deed.


src:VERBS-6: Yes, I added that comment. This is *not* the original verbs.zil file.
	It’s very close, though. Only a few changes were made between r27 and r28, and I’m pretty confident that I reverted them correctly.

src:VERBS-1467: Why did they remove this `RTRUE` in r28? It appears to be an attempt to save space.
	If you reach the end of a ZIL routine without an explicit return statement, it returns the last value expression. In this case that’s `<SETG QCONTEXT-ROOM ,HERE>`, which is an object number. Object numbers are always positive, so you can save a line of code and still effectively return true.
	What the developer didn’t realize is that returning an arbitrary number takes two bytes of Z-code, whereas `RTRUE` only takes one! The “optimization” in r28 saves a line of code but *wastes* a byte in the game file.

src:VERBS-1732: Why did they remove this `RTRUE` in r28? It appears to be an attempt to save space.
	If you reach the end of a ZIL routine without an explicit return statement, it returns the last value expression. In this case that’s `<SETG HERE .OHERE>`, which is an object number. Object numbers are always positive, so you can save a line of code and still effectively return true.
	What the developer didn’t realize is that returning an arbitrary number takes two bytes of Z-code, whereas `RTRUE` only takes one! The “optimization” in r28 saves a line of code but *wastes* a byte in the game file.

src:VERBS-290: This wry comment does not appear to have ever been used in a released version of the game.

rtn:V-ZORK: The `ZORK` command was removed from the grammar early on; no known release recognizes it. This vestige remains.

obj:STAIRS: This room lists itself as a scenery object (the `GLOBAL` property). This doesn’t seem to have any effect, but it doesn’t hurt the game either. It threw my Visible Zorker code for an (infinite) loop though!

rtn:PORTRAITS-F: Phillips is Mrs. Robner’s maiden name (see [here|~src:ACTIONS-1696]). I suspect it is also a very backhanded Zork reference: the Phillips family is the counterpart of the Flatheads.
